```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on srv1 itself, not in the switching or routing infrastructure.
The entire network path from PC1 (10.1.0.100) through aswa2 → dswa1/dswa2 →
csw1/csw2 → dswb1/dswb2 → aswb3 → srv1 (10.5.0.4) is intact: ICMP ping
succeeded throughout, and TCP RSTs were being returned by srv1 (not dropped
in transit), proving Layer 3 forwarding was healthy.

The affected component is the srv1 startup script
(`/hostlab/srv1.startup`). In Kathara, each device is initialised solely by
its startup script; the SysV rc2.d/rc3.d symlinks (S01apache2, S01ssh) are
present in the container image but are never invoked because Kathara does not
run a full init daemon. The startup script, however, was modified so that it
only starts BIND9 (`named`). The commands to start Apache 2.4 (`service
apache2 start`) and OpenSSH (`service ssh start`) were removed. Consequently,
when the lab was last brought up (overnight), srv1 came up with no
user-accessible services: every TCP connection attempt was answered with a
RST (connection refused), which end users experience as the server being
unreachable.

Manually issuing `service apache2 start` and `service ssh start` on srv1
immediately restored connectivity — PC1 connected to port 80 and port 22
without any routing or ACL changes.

Root cause: `srv1.startup` is missing the `service apache2 start` and
`service ssh start` lines, so neither the web server nor SSH daemon starts
when the lab initialises.

Corrective fix: Add `service apache2 start` and `service ssh start` to
`/hostlab/srv1.startup` (before the `named` call or after it, order does not
matter). This ensures both services are present every time the lab or the
container is restarted.
=== END ===
```